    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">A search space is a graph (see Figure 5) consisting of hyperparameters (either of type independent or dependent) and modules (either of type basic or substitution).</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">perposition of all possible child models in a search space of NAS</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">an additional dimension represented by the neural architecture is added to the search space.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Hyperopt-Sklearn uses Hyperopt [11] to dene the search space over the possible Scikit-Learn main components</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">The larger search space expands the hypothesis space H</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">The search space geometry has a direct inuence on the kernel K ( x , x ) of the Gaussian Process surrogate model</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">current implementations of architecture search methods rely on ad-hoc encodings for search spaces</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">The volume doubling method [ 16 ] can continue to expand the search space to innity</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Applying Bayesian optimization in problems wherein the search space is unknown is challenging.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">good learning configuration in the search space</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">the general search space is complex, containing various learning configurations with different value ranges.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">hierarchical search space</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">output a search space X X</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">the search space structured by DAG can be applied. For search space structured by DAG, the most typical application is neural architecture search</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">search space corresponds to range of A</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">designing and encoding such search spaces</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">our search space is much larger than that of single-model AutoML systems</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">ENAS + macro search space</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Bayesian optimization in problems wherein the search space is unknown</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">a conguration space containing the possible combinations of algorithms and hyperparameters is dened.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">In the larger space, 32 trials were necessary to consistently outperform grid search rather than 8</hasEvidenceText>
  </rdf:Description>
