    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Bayesian optimization is a sample-efcient approach to global optimization that relies on theoretically motivated value heuristics (acquisition functions) to guide its search process.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Bayesian optimization (BO) is a model-based approach for gradient-free black-box function optimization, such as hyperparameter optimization.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Bayesian optimization method</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">uses Bayesian Optimization (Sequential model-based optimization) to search for optimal hyperparameter settings</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">complex model tuning techniques, such as Bayesian optimization or evolutionary algorithm.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">uses Bayesian Optimization (Sequential model-based optimization) to search for optimal hyperparameter settings of the pipeline.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">resulting in a novel Bayesian optimization algorithm whose target is moving with the iteration number.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">BO models balance the exploration and the exploitation processes to detect the current most likely optimal regions</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Bayesian optimization (BO) is a successful methodology to optimize black-box functions that are expensive to evaluate.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">the third design choice of the Bayesian Optimization pipeline, often overlooked, is the search space.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Bayesian optimization is a sample-efcient approach to global optimization</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">BO search space, which is a critical input to any BO procedure applied to HPO</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">GP based methods were found to perform best on these lowdimensional benchmarks, which we thus take as a point of reference.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">GP-based BO cannot leverage large numbers of past function evaluations, for example, to warm-start related BO run</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Gray-Box Bayesian Optimization method for both model selection and hyperparameter search</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Bayesian optimization is a prominent method for optimizing expensive-to-evaluate black-box functions that is widely applied to tuning the hyperparameters of machine learning algorithms.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Bayesian Optimization is one of the state-of-the-art blackbox optimization techniques which is tailored for expensive objective functions</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">use their result to seed Bayesian optimization. This approach of warmstarting optimization by meta-learning has already been successfully applied</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">These approaches are fundamentally Bayesian in nature, hence this literature goes under the name Bayesian Optimization</hasEvidenceText>
  </rdf:Description>
